\ the Hurricane Mk I: the fuselage of ten-sided rings from the cowling to the rudder's hinge, deep to its end, and the keel along its underside from the tail wheel to the rudder's lower corner, the spinner and the turning propeller's disc, three long exhaust stubs a side, 18 cm long and 12 cm high, solid and standing 9 cm out from the cowling with top and bottom faces, where the Watts three-view draws them, the hood and spine, the wings, the radiator under the fuselage, a tub with a U-shaped section deepest behind its intake, painted Sky, the tailplane and elevators, the fin and rudder, the ventral keel under the rear fuselage, in Sky, and the fixed tail wheel, the aerial mast and its wire, the roundels on the upper wings, each tilted to its wing's dihedral, and on the fuselage's sides; the undercarriage when down. Filled as the Spitfire is, in Dark Green with its own Dark Earth patches, Sky below, the spinner black
