	) each ;

\ the cowling and the fuselage as two bodies of rings on the thrust line, each ring's halves meeting at the longerons and reaching its top and bottom, the fuselage round throughout, its rings ellipses ahead of the hood with their widest point 0.1 m above the thrust line so the top is a full arch like the cowling's, and from the hood aft superellipses of exponent 3, round on top with slightly flattened sides, their widest point near the thrust line: the Mercury's cowling 1.36 m across, its lip 2.38 m forward, its rear 1.63 m forward, its rear face painted dark where it overhangs the fuselage; the fuselage 1.08 m wide at first and 1.15 m under the hood, its deck a barrel curve, steepest at the cowling and flattening aft, from 0.60 m up just behind the cowling through 0.66 m at 1.40 m forward, 0.69 m at 1.20 m and 0.73 m at 0.78 m to 0.74 m at the screen's apex, 6 cm above the cowling's top, then falling to 0.62 m at the hood's front hoop, the sill's own height, so the windscreen's glass runs into it at an angle and the cockpit's opening is cut down to the sills along the hood's length; the underside the same curve mirrored, from 0.47 m down just behind the cowling, 15 cm inside the cowling's bottom, through 0.55 m, 0.58 m and 0.61 m down to 0.62 m at the windscreen, level with the cowling's bottom, and level on to 0.61 m down at the hood's rear, then a straight line to 0.21 m down at the sternpost; the decking behind the hood a straight line from the sill height, 0.62 m up at the rear window's frame, to the fin's foot 0.44 m up 3.75 m aft, the longerons sloping with it; heights from the side view, widths from the plan and underside views
